{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md | sed -n '107,136p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json | sed -n '86,96p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json | rg '85%|1.85|85 percent' | cut -c1-2300\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_31","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '782,795p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md | sed -n '35,46p' | cut -c1-1200\"","aggregated_output":"   782\tdef publish_decision(family, calc, result):\n   783\t    \"\"\"'refused' when a check fails; 'held' when a member is unresolved and a declared relation joins it to another member; else\n   784\t    'publish' (an unresolved member is shown as unresolved).\"\"\"\n   785\t    if result['errors']:\n   786\t        return 'refused'\n   787\t    unresolved = {a['view_id'] for a in calc['answers'] if a.get('shape') == 'unresolved'}\n   788\t    for rel in family.get('relations', []):\n   789\t        ends = {rel.get('part'), rel.get('whole'), rel.get('later'), rel.get('earlier')} - {None}\n   790\t        if ends & unresolved and ends - unresolved:\n   791\t            return 'held'\n   792\t    return 'publish'\n    35\t\n    36\t# The two readings\n    37\t- Reader 1 on member v6: supported = no. Ground: The forecast subtracts an estimated $25 billion of spending outside data centers from $745 billion of total capex, producing $720 billion in rung 4. The total draws on e5014, e1856, and e2458. The deduction rests on e1947 and a comparison of Meta's AI capex estimate in e3686 with its total capex guidance in e1549. Why not: At a $745 billion total, rung 4 requires spending outside data centers to be at most $45 billion. The four-company AI infrastructure tally in e1947 does not break out data-center capex, and Meta's spending mix cannot establish a bound for all four companies. The broader estimates in e1416 and e4082 do not isolate these four, and e5376 covers five without sizing the fifth company's share.\n    38\t- Reader 1 marks no defect.\n    39\t- Reader 2 on member v6: supported = yes. Ground: F13 = F11 total capex $745bn minus F12 $25bn spending outside data centers = $720bn, in rung 4 [700, 800). The $745bn total rests on the four-company tallies: e5014 ($720-745bn, top end), e1856 (near $745bn), e2458 ($760bn) and e1947 (more than $730bn of AI infrastructure). The band is decided by the deduction, which must stay below about $45bn at a $745bn total. The passages bound it as small. e1947's more than $730bn of AI infrastructure for the same four companies sits close to the total. Meta's AI capex of $130bn (e3686) against its $125-145bn guidance (e1549, midpoint $135bn) implies about 4% outside AI, roughly $28bn on $745bn. Figures labelled as data-center spending, e4082 and e1416 (about $750bn), also put it above $700bn. The margin is thin: at the Zacks low of $720bn, a Meta-like deduction lands near $693bn. Still, e1947's floor of more than $730bn on AI infrastructure supports rung 4.\n    40\t- Reader 2 marks no defect.\n    41\t\n    42\t# The system text the writer was given\n    43\tYou are a senior hedge-fund analyst judging the current rungs of a family of related number theses: one company's quantities that overlap, such as a total and its segment, a level and its growth, or a later year that rests on an earlier year's base. Use only the supplied evidence and themes in force. Each rung is your forecast: the outcome the evidence in force makes most likely at the due date, not the one it has proved.\n    44\t\n    45\t- Judge every member from the evidence shown (what recent judgements relied on and what arrived recently) and the themes in force, as if for the first time. Earlier forecasts are not shown: whether a conclusion changed is compared afterwards.\n    46\t- Judge each member against a reference: what the market expects for that question (consensus, the company's guidance, the stated plan or deadline). Say where the reference sits on its ladder. A consensus or guidance figure by itself keeps the judgement at the reference's rung; once other evidence has arrived it is never the reason to stay. Then derive the outcome the evidence implies: reported figures and guidance give a run-rate whose pace you forecast, informed by guidance, never the last quarter annualised flat; a quantified revision, a customer's demand number, a supplier's constraint, a confirmed delay or cancellation shift it. A target for a product with no shipments yet is discounted subject to the evidence requirement below; it is taken in full only when the evidence shows the volume in hand. Build the family's figures once and read every member's outcome from them: a part cannot exceed its whole, a figure that spans several periods or products gives a question only its share, and a quantity the family shares has one forecast. Compare each outcome with its rung boundaries, and move when it crosses one.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_32","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md | sed -n '107,136p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json | sed -n '86,96p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json | rg '85%|1.85|85 percent' | cut -c1-2300\"","aggregated_output":"   107\t  - All five controls passed: K-midpoint was not marked; K-misread was marked by both readers; K-overlap was not marked; K-disjoint was\n   108\t    marked by both; K-unsupported was \"not supported\" by both.\n   109\t  - On the 12 replies, no defect was marked by both readers. The class from §4 (format-permitted forms marked) did not recur.\n   110\t- **STOP on two registered lines, as written.**\n   111\t  1. **A settled defect on an accepted reply: T10 (S, Claude writer), F11.** e1416 writes \"an estimated $750 billion\". The writer\n   112\t     recorded qualifier \"none\", and both adjudicators upheld \"number misread\". But \"estimated\" is not in the qualifier list the schema\n   113\t     enumerates (`calc_small.QUALIFIERS`), so \"none\" was the only value the format allowed. The descriptions the adjudicators saw say\n   114\t     \"the qualifier the quote writes just before the number\" and do not list the allowed values. This is a format gap: the closed list\n   115\t     and the description disagree.\n   116\t  2. **A case with zero usable first replies: S.**\n   117\t     - S's Codex reply is \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 90% data-center share decides rung 3, and the passages do not size the\n   118\t       share.\n   119\t     - S's Claude reply is split at both levels. Its 95–100% share sets the range, and Codex says the passages do not support 95% as\n   120\t       the low end.\n   121\t     - Both writers met D741 ruling 3's letter (a stated deduction), but not its \"supported\" part. That is a judgement weakness of the\n   122\t       writers on this question, caught by the readers, not an instrument error.\n   123\t- **Other settled support:**\n   124\t  - E's Codex reply is now \"not supported\" by both readers. Its 15% cut from the guided $40B floor decides rung 3 without passage\n   125\t    support. Earlier readers had supported it, and the two-step brief (name the evidence for the band-setting assumption) made readers\n   126\t    stricter on such cuts.\n   127\t  - O's Codex reply is supported after adjudication; O's Claude reply is split again.\n   128\t- **Usable replies per case:**\n   129\t\n   130\t  | Case | Usable replies |\n   131\t  |---|---|\n   132\t  | P | both writers |\n   133\t  | P-v4 | both writers |\n   134\t  | P-v8 | both writers |\n   135\t  | O | Codex |\n   136\t  | E | Claude |\n    86\t      \"low\": null,\n    87\t      \"high\": null,\n    88\t      \"unit\": \"%\",\n    89\t      \"evidence_id\": null,\n    90\t      \"quote_number\": null,\n    91\t      \"qualifier\": null,\n    92\t      \"inputs\": null,\n    93\t      \"operation\": null,\n    94\t      \"basis_text\": \"I estimate that NVIDIA realizes 90% of the reference. The announced breadth of deployments supports most of the ramp. Anthropic is still evaluating Vera, and AMD's agentic-workload tests show a competitive alternative. I allow for some planned sales to slip or go to a rival, equivalent to less than half an average quarter of revenue at the target pace. The passages give no order sizes or shipment pace to narrow that estimate.\",\n    95\t      \"rests_on\": [\"F3\", \"e4689\", \"e5044\", \"e5403\"],\n    96\t      \"outcome_for\": [],\n   131\t      \"basis_text\": \"My estimate of FY2028 Vera CPU revenue growth on the FY2027 base. Management's 'more than double' (F2, at least 100% growth) is the benchmark, but it is a target for a fiscal year that has not started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full. Outside views put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD, F4; Bank of America's fivefold path from F5 to F6), so a doubling needs large share gains. Broad announced adoption (every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and OEM, e4689; Anthropic evaluating Vera, e5044; Arm servers' large revenue share, e4692) supports a pace well above the market, while AMD's own benchmark claims against Vera (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404) show standalone CPU share will be contested. Weighting the company target most heavily, tempered toward the market pace, gives 85%.\",\n   162\t      \"reasons\": \"Reference: NVIDIA's own guidance. CFO Colette Kress reaffirmed about $20 billion of CPU revenue for FY2027 (F1, e3660) and said FY2028 would more than double it (F2, e4856); NVIDIA's wording ties both the base and the doubling to Vera CPU revenue (e4691, e4856). That gives more than $40 billion (F3), the bottom edge of rung 2 (Bullish, $40-50 billion). Forecast: this is a target for a full fiscal year that has not yet started, for a product line still ramping, so it is discounted for time and execution rather than taken in full; the whole of FY2028 is still to be sold. Outside benchmarks put CPU market growth near 50% a year (AMD's server CPU estimate, F4; Bank of America's path from $35 billion to $170 billion, F5 and F6), roughly half the pace NVIDIA needs, so a doubling requires large share gains. Adoption breadth supports a pace well above the market: Kress says every major hyperscaler, neocloud, AI lab and system OEM will deploy Vera (e4689), Anthropic is evaluating it for CPU-heavy agentic work (e5044), and Arm-based servers already hold a large share of data-center revenue (e4692). Against that, AMD's own benchmarks claim its Venice and EPYC 9996 parts beat Vera per core, per platform, per rack and in memory bandwidth (e5399, e5400, e5401, e5402, e5404); these are a competitor's marketing tests from one article and are weighed lightly, but they show standalone CPU share will be contested. The CEO's and other outlets' repeats of the doubling (e4764, e4691, e4856) add no new fact. The 2027 hyperscaler capex theme, upgraded to the upper-middle range, supports strong AI infrastructure demand in the year that matters but does not by itself imply a doubling of CPU revenue. I forecast 85% growth (F7) on the $20 billion base, giving $37 billion (F8): rung 3 (Neutral), just below the rung-2 line. It would move to rung 2 if FY2027 lands at or above $20 billion with Vera Rubin shipping on schedule, or if NVIDIA sizes FY2028 Vera orders; a confirmed Rubin delay or an FY2027 shortfall would push it toward rung 4.\",\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11236,11276p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-adjudication/T10.md | rg -n 'two readings|Reader 1 on member|Reader 2 on member' | cut -c1-260\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_33","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/DECISIONS-log.md | sed -n '11236,11276p'; nl -ba poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/reread2-adjudication/T10.md | rg -n 'two readings|Reader 1 on member|Reader 2 on member' | cut -c1-260\"","aggregated_output":" 11236\t## D737 · 2026-11-22 · Alex: shrink judging-v13's format to the checks that stop the named defects, then a $0 blind test\n 11237\t\n 11238\t- **The question** (after D736): \"判数字新版本的修补连续三轮审查都在冒新规则（填写说明将从 1 万字涨到约 2 万字，每个答案约 16 个数、每个数要同时满足几十条规则）。Codex 主张修完再审最后一轮；我主张现在改结构。下一步怎么走？\"\n 11239\t  Options:\n 11240\t  - 砍成小格式再盲测 (recommended): keep only the checks that stop five defects (a number misread, wrong arithmetic, a part\n 11241\t    above its whole or added into it, two forecasts of one quarter, an own estimate passed off as the market's); the other rules\n 11242\t    become assumptions shown to readers; council, then the $0 blind test, then a request for the paid re-send;\n 11243\t  - 按原计划再修一轮 (Codex's position);\n 11244\t  - 关掉这条线.\n 11245\t- **Alex's answer** (2026-11-22), the recommended option (\"…成小格式再盲测 (推荐)\").\n 11246\t- **Decided (Alex):**\n 11247\t  - The judging-v13 format shrinks. Code refuses an answer only for the five defects above and for broken structure (links that do\n 11248\t    not resolve, a cycle, a missing or doubled answer, an outcome not of the question's quantity, a rung that does not hold the\n 11249\t    outcome).\n 11250\t  - Each such rule is written in one or two sentences beside its field. Every other rule of DESIGN.md's checks becomes an assumption\n 11251\t    recorded and shown to the readers, never a refusal.\n 11252\t  - The council reviews within that scope. A counterexample that lets none of the five defects through is a reader's matter, not a\n 11253\t    finding.\n 11254\t  - Then the $0 blind test (REPAIR-DESIGN.md §5, with round 51's reader fix). A clean result returns to Alex for the paid re-send under\n 11255\t    D725's lines.\n 11256\t- **Superseded:** REPAIR-DESIGN.md v2's full restatement (D735's finish line), replaced by the small format.\n 11257\t\n 11258\t## D738 · 2026-11-22 · v13s: code refuses a named defect only where the calculation's structure decides it; readers mark the five defects on every reply (council rounds 52–53)\n 11259\t\n 11260\t- **Rounds 52 and 53** (`research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r52.md`, `codex-r53.md`) reviewed the small format within D737's\n 11261\t  scope. Each closing rule written for an indirect form of a named defect produced new counterexamples in both directions:\n 11262\t  - \"uses the part\" lineage refuses a correct sum of two Data Center quarters, each computed as a share of company revenue;\n 11263\t  - \"one figure per quantity\" refuses two outside estimates averaged into one starting point;\n 11264\t  - multiplying by 1, relabelling a basis, or routing through an estimate's judgement still passes.\n 11265\t- **Settled (how D737's rule is built; D737 says code refuses only for the five defects, not that code catches every instance):**\n 11266\t  - Code refuses a named defect where the calculation's own structure decides it without reading intent:\n 11267\t    - **S8:** a sum whose inputs include the part's quantity and the whole's quantity themselves.\n 11268\t    - **S4:** two figures with basis forecast for one quantity and period, or a figure of a listed shared quantity used by an outcome\n 11269\t      on a path that bypasses its forecast figure.\n 11270\t    - **S3:** a part above its whole, compared with paired ends when the part is computed from the whole and lowest against highest\n 11271\t      otherwise, on recomputed values. A share takes 0 to 100 percent.\n 11272\t    - **S5:** an estimate or forecast at or beneath the marked reference.\n 11273\t    - **S1, S2, S6, S7** as written.\n 11274\t  - Indirect forms (a multiplier of one, a relabelled basis, an estimate's judgement) are recorded for readers. They are never\n 11275\t    refused.\n 11276\t  - **The readers** in the blind test and the gate mark the five defects on every reply. In the blind test, an accepted reply with\n33:    33\t# The two readings\n34:    34\t- Reader 1 on member v6: supported = no. Ground: F6 estimates $175 billion in Microsoft capital expenditure using e922, e4405 and outside estimates of the four companies’ total spending. F13 assumes 95–100% of combined capital expenditure goes to dat\n36:    36\t- Reader 2 on member v6: supported = yes. Ground: Build of the four companies' 2026 capital expenditure from the shown guidance: Alphabet $195-205bn (e1254, F1), Meta $125-145bn (e1549, F2), Amazon $220bn (e1649, F3), plus a stated estimate for Micro\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_34","type":"agent_message","text":"**Ask Alex whether an indirect comparison can justify publishing the more likely forecast. I lean yes. Keep the paid re-send on hold.** The $36B Vera answer shows why these are separate decisions. Its writer acknowledged that the passages gave no order size or shipment pace, then used a $4B shortfall to put sales below $40B. Both first readers rejected that answer. Resolving every disagreement about cloud spending would still leave D745 failed. [Vera answer:94](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/codex.json:94) · [Recorded votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Registered pass conditions:375](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:375)\n\n1. **Agree with Claude’s revised lean. I dispute two of the reasons given for it.** The writer is asked for the most likely outcome. The frozen examples make that concrete. For q19, the price per CPU tells the reader that the target implies four million units in roughly two selling quarters. That supports a discount even though no passage supplies the final $8–18B range. For q04, the reader weighs spending plans, demand and delays. D741 still applies when a writer starts from total spending to forecast a narrower amount. It must state the deduction or give a supported bound. The open choice is whether an indirect comparison can support the stated deduction strongly enough to pick a side. Alex has not already approved that threshold. D725 does not promise that code catches every invented or mislabelled figure. And §8’s “no data to verify” concerns checking outcomes afterward. It gives no permission to publish an unsupported estimate today. [Writer instructions:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/system.txt:1) · [q19:897](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:897) · [q04:246](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-01-e2e-loop/judgement-gold/gold-v1.json:246) · [D741:11359](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11359) · [Limits of the code checks:11265](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11265) · [AGENTS.md:416](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:416)\n\n2. **Agree on asking one general question. Disagree with wording that assumes the related number already points to the answer.** The four companies said they expected to spend more than $730B on *AI infrastructure* in 2026. The question asks about *data-center capital spending*. A writer who starts from $745B of total spending has to explain why spending outside data centers is likely to be at most $45B to reach the $700B cutoff. That gives Alex a concrete choice without asking him to define “AI infrastructure” for all future cases. [Spending passages:27](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/S/user.txt:27) · [Two readings of the $45B difference:36](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U08.md:36)\n\n   **My question for Alex:** “资料没直接给出所问的数。用一个相关数字作比较，能解释为什么结果更可能在线的这一边，但另一边仍有可能。这时可以发布预测，还是要等到能从资料算出结果落在线的哪一边？”\n\n   - **可以发布（我推荐）。** 要讲清这个比较为什么适用于所问的公司、时间和数字，以及按答案自己的假设，为什么这一边更可能。\n   - **先说‘还判断不了’。** 等资料能直接给出或推算出所问的数在线的哪一边，再发布预测。\n\n   This covers using market growth as a comparison for one vendor. It does not make market growth a guaranteed minimum. Both readers accepted the earlier $37B Vera answer, which explained why NVIDIA might outgrow the market. The later answer added a 90% Vera share. At its lower growth estimate, $20B × 1.5 × 90% gives **$27B**. The writer itself acknowledges that sensitivity. The reader who claimed the whole range stayed at $30–40B dropped the writer’s assumption. That error is settled by arithmetic. Whether the writer gave enough reason to prefer its $32.4B forecast was still a split vote. [Earlier Vera reasoning:131](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/E/answers/claude.json:131) · [T15 votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later Vera answer:162](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/answers/claude.json:162) · [Adjudicator’s claim:1](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/adjudication/U06.claude.json:1)\n\n3. **Agree with the stored-answer re-read as the one next test.** After Alex rules, keep the answers fixed and change only the shared reading instructions to include his words. The synthetic passage in round 61 would show whether a fresh writer can use an explicit bound. It would tell us little about why two readers looking at the *same* answer disagree. Claude’s list contains **eight distinct answers** because U04 is both a split and a control. I would add T15 as the real comparison for U06. That gives **nine answers, 18 independent first readings, zero new writer calls and $0 in paid sends**. T10 is there for its spending argument only. Its previously confirmed quotation error stands. [Round 61 proposal:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/codex-r61.md:32) · [U04 and other controls:4](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:4) · [T10’s separate error:111](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND3-RESULT.md:111)\n\n   **What would move the decision:** Before reading, record what Alex’s choice implies for the examples and controls. Move on to writer repair if both families independently apply that choice to the spending forecasts and the “can’t tell” answer, reject the unsupported Vera cuts, and judge T15 and U06 using each writer’s actual assumptions. Matching votes for incompatible reasons do not settle the disagreement. A continuing split leaves the publication question unresolved in practice. Even a clean re-read cannot turn D745 into a pass. [Shared reading rule:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/blind_rules.py:15) · [D741’s sequence:11364](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11364)\n\n4. **Agree on waiting for an E repair. Disagree that a paid STOP is already predictable.** Of four Vera answers, **two were rejected by both readers, one split, and one was accepted by both**. Three contained a cut or share whose size is in question. Those four answers came from Codex and Claude. They give no measured failure rate for Luna, and the free readers voted on support while the paid readers separately mark whether a gap decided the band. Paid S also asks about **2027**. The stored S answers concern **2026**. [Earlier Vera votes:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind3-2026-11-22/REREAD2-TALLY.json:169) · [Later votes:8](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/TALLY.json:8) · [Paid cases:15](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:15) · [Free cases:169](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:169)\n\n   **The attainable paid verdict is conditional.** On a complete run, one confirmed deciding gap in any of the three published Vera answers forces **STOP**, even if joint judging wins all nine pairs. Codex breaks a reader tie. With zero published defects across all joint and singleton answers, **PASS** remains possible with **8 joint wins and at most 5 separate wins**, or **9 joint wins and at most 6 separate wins**, with at least two usable joint pairs per snapshot. A properly explained “can’t tell” is published for a singleton and is still inspected. So the 36 calls could measure Luna and the benefit of judging related questions together. The estimated $0.17 cost and proposed $0.50 ceiling do not override the requirement for a clean free test before requesting authorization. [Paid reading and verdict:262](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:262) · [Verdict calculation:772](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/paid_gate.py:772) · [Singleton publication:782](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py:782) · [Cost estimate:32](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/PAID-GATE.md:32) · [Authorization condition:380](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/SMALL-DESIGN.md:380)\n\n   **The smallest writer change I would take forward after the ruling** is to make the writer choose between the existing “forecast” and “can’t tell” answers *before* it assigns a discount. At that choice, it must identify what makes one side more likely under its own assumptions. If it can explain only why sales might slip, it can report that gap directly. This is a proposed repair. D742 addressed a different problem involving quarterly guidance. D745 is the round that directly tried and failed to fix unsupported discounts. Automatically publishing management’s number whenever it sits on the cutoff would change the product and would require Alex’s separate decision. [Existing answer choices:287](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/poc/data/judging-v13s-blind4-2026-11-23/E/schema.json:287) · [D742:11397](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11397) · [D745:11513](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:11513) · [Recurring failures:85](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:85)"}}
